            "**1** For thousands of years, people have left records of their lives without writing a single word. Archaeologists, the scientists who study the human past, have learned to read these records in the objects and traces people leave behind.\n\n"
            "**2** Some of the oldest records in Australia are shell middens. A midden is a mound built up over many generations from the remains of meals, mostly shells, along with ash from cooking fires, bones and broken stone tools. To a passer-by it may look like a heap of rubbish. To an archaeologist, and to the Aboriginal communities whose ancestors made it, it is a detailed history.\n\n"
            "**3** Each layer of a midden tells part of the story. The kinds of shells reveal which animals were eaten and which parts of the coast were used. A change in the shells from one layer to the next can show that the climate or the shoreline changed. Charcoal from the fires can be dated in a laboratory, giving the age of each layer.\n\n"
            "**4** Middens are fragile. Storms, rising seas, building work and even people walking over them can wear them away. Once a layer is disturbed, the order of the story is lost and cannot be put back. For this reason, middens are protected by law, and archaeologists today work closely with Traditional Owners to decide how each site should be cared for.\n\n"
            "**5** The lesson of the midden is that history is not only found in books. Sometimes it is written in shells, waiting for someone who knows how to read it."
