            "**1** Some of the oldest buildings in the world are made from mud. That might sound like a joke, but mud brick, also called adobe, is one of the most widely used building materials in history, and it is still used in many countries today, including Australia.\n\n"
            "**2** Making mud bricks is simple. Clay-rich soil is mixed with water and often with straw, which helps stop the bricks from cracking as they dry. The mixture is pressed into wooden moulds, turned out and left to dry in the sun for several weeks. No kiln or fuel is needed.\n\n"
            "**3** Mud brick walls are usually thick and heavy. During a hot day, they soak up heat slowly, so the inside of the house stays cool. At night, the stored heat is slowly released, keeping the rooms warm. This ability to store heat and release it later is called thermal mass.\n\n"
            "**4** Mud brick does have weaknesses. It can be worn away by heavy rain, so builders protect it with wide roof overhangs and a stone or concrete base that keeps the bottom of the walls dry. It is also slow to make, and building rules about it vary from place to place.\n\n"
            "**5** Still, as people look for ways to build homes that use less energy, this ancient material is getting a second look. Sometimes the best new idea turns out to be a very old one."
