            "**1** In many parts of the world, autumn turns whole forests yellow, orange and red. In Australia, most native trees keep their leaves all year, but you can see the same show in parks and streets planted with trees from the northern hemisphere, such as maples and oaks.\n\n"
            "**2** Leaves are green because they contain a substance called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll helps the leaf turn sunlight, water and air into food for the tree. While the tree is growing, it keeps making new chlorophyll, so the leaves stay green.\n\n"
            "**3** As autumn days grow shorter and cooler, some trees prepare to rest for winter. They stop making chlorophyll, and the green colour breaks down. Yellow and orange colours, which were in the leaf all along, can now be seen. Some trees also make new red colours in their leaves at this time.\n\n"
